WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:07.690
At the end of the last section,
I gave you a 4 x 4 distance matrix,

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:10.750
and I asked you to apply
the algorithm from the last section

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:12.737
in order to reconstruct
the simple tree fitting it.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.270
I hope that you were driven
crazy by this exercise.

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:19.750
Things were probably
coming along just fine,

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:22.250
And then you encountered an edge
with negative weight, and

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:23.010
everything went haywire.

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:28.610
Maybe you think I was mean enough to
give you a non-additive matrix, but

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:31.460
there does exist a simple
tree fitting this matrix.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:32.970
If you don't believe me, here it is.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.279
So there must be something wrong
with our proposed algorithm.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.420
If we look at the matrix itself,

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.560
then the minimum element is supposed
to correspond to neighbors j and k.

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:49.530
But there's just one tiny problem
with this, which is that j and

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:50.840
k are not neighbors in the tree.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:56.980
So hopefully you will see why I tricked
you with leading us down the wrong path.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:00.750
When we're designing an algorithm,
we must be mathematically rigorous.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:05.000
Just because a statement may seem to be
true, doesn't necessarily make it true.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.250
And you will see later that leading you
down the wrong path on a search for

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:11.730
neighbors wasn't completely
a lost cause either.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.100
For now, let's not lose hope for

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:20.400
a method that will reconstruct a tree
that fits an additive distance matrix.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.980
The idea that we had of using
recursion was a good idea,

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:27.180
but rather than trying to reduce the size
of the tree by finding a pair of

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:29.280
neighbors, let's instead try and

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.420
compute the length of limbs or the edges
that are connected to a tree's leaves.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.320
Now, we found a formula for the length
of a limb in the previous section,

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.750
but that was assuming that i and
j were neighbors.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.570
However, this formula is
at least in the ballpark.

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.840
To compute the limb length of i, we will
need to take the minimum value of this

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:00.320
expression as we let j and
k range over all leaves of the tree.

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:04.310
If you're interested in seeing a proof
of this theorem, just see the main text.

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:10.280
So, note that here we denote
the length of the limb of j

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:11.930
using the notation LimbLength(j).

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:14.960
Now that we have this theorem,

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.280
you can use it compute the length
of any limb of the tree,

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:21.160
which we can state as a computational
problem and you can code yourself.

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:25.243
When we return to our toy distance matrix,

00:02:25.243 --> 00:02:28.130
let's pretend that we don't
know what the tree looks like.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.537
We can substitute chimp for
i here in the Limb Length Theorem.

00:02:35.422 --> 00:02:38.850
Now we just need to compute the formula
in the Limb Length Theorem for

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:40.710
all other choices of leaves j and k.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:43.750
We can take these leaves to be human and

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:47.165
seal, in which case when we plug
in the matrix values, we get 1.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:52.180
We could take these leaves to be human and
whale,

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:56.830
in which case we get 1 again when
we plug in the matrix values.

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:58.780
Or we could take these
leaves to be whale and

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:02.390
seal, in which case we get 4 when
we plug in the matrix values.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.955
So the minimum of these three values is 1.

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:12.170
And that confirms what we already
knew was the limb length of chimp.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.429
Now we're ready to introduce
a recursive algorithm called

00:03:18.429 --> 00:03:22.250
AdditivePhylogeny that operates
by computing limb length.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:27.380
So let me show you the outline of how this
algorithm works on the additive matrix

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:30.180
that we encountered at the end of the last
section and that I tripped you up on.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:35.320
We begin by selecting an arbitrary leaf,
say j.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.920
We can then apply the Limb Length Theorem
to find that the length of this leaf,

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.170
excuse me, the length of this limb,

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:47.798
which in this case
is going to be equal to 2.

00:03:47.798 --> 00:03:52.465
We can form a matrix Dbald by
then subtracting the limb length

00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:56.285
from each entry in the j-th row and
column of D.

00:03:56.285 --> 00:03:59.300
We will see the purpose of
doing this subtraction soon.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:03.090
But note that what we're doing is really
compressing j towards its parent so

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:05.590
that the limb of j has length 0.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:06.730
This is called a "bald limb".

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:12.460
So we'll hang onto j for later, but
for now, let's remove the j-th row and

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:16.585
column from the bald matrix to form
a smaller matrix called Dtrim.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.840
We will then call AdditivePhylogeny
on this smaller matrix

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.980
to reconstruct the simple tree
that fits this trimmed matrix.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:31.510
Now, to add j back to the tree,
we must identify the point

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:35.580
in the simple tree fitting Dtrim
where j attaches to the tree.

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:41.870
Once we find this point,
we can attach j by an edge of length 2,

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:44.740
the limb length that we
computed previously.

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:48.840
So here is a summary of AdditivePhylogeny.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:54.684
You may want to take a moment
to review it before we continue.

00:04:54.684 --> 00:04:56.559
If you look at this carefully, and

00:04:56.559 --> 00:04:59.085
if you were paying close
attention to what I was saying,

00:04:59.085 --> 00:05:02.527
then each step of this algorithm
may have been clear, except for

00:05:02.527 --> 00:05:06.050
step 6, where we need to
identify the attachment point,

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:12.780
to put j back in the tree of
the trimmed matrix to form the tree of D.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:16.910
This is where that bald
matrix should come in handy.

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:21.699
So remember that Dbald was formed by
subtracting the limb length of j from each

00:05:21.699 --> 00:05:24.580
element in the j-th row and
j-th column of D.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:30.460
So we'll apply the Limb Length Theorem
then, to this bald matrix,

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:35.530
on the length of the limb of j. But

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:40.810
we know that the length of the limb
of j in the bald matrix is just 0.

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:46.544
And so when we take this minimum,
we see that we obtain

00:05:46.544 --> 00:05:53.320
that Dbald_i,j plus Dbald_j,k minus Dbald_i,k
over 2 is going to be equal to 0,

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:57.280
because 11 plus 10 minus 21 is equal to 0.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:01.524
So if we move Dbald_i,k to the
right side of this equation,

00:06:01.524 --> 00:06:06.910
this means that Dbald_i,k is equal to
the sum of Dbald_i,j and Dbald_j,k.

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:10.250
So how do we interpret this formula?

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:12.980
Well, it means that the attachment
point for

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:16.610
j must occur somewhere
along the path from i to k.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:21.440
And specifically, it means that
this attachment point must occur at

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:26.380
distance Dbald_i,j,
which is equal to 11, from leaf i.

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:33.660
So, this time the attachment point occurred
along an edge of the tree, meaning that

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:37.720
we can form a new internal node along
that edge at the attachment point.

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:40.050
But I want you to note that
the attachment point for

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:45.440
j could theoretically have occurred at
an existing internal node of this tree,

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:45.970
in which case

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:49.870
we simply need to just join
the limb of j onto this existing node.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:54.116
So, now that know how to
find the attachment point,

00:06:54.116 --> 00:06:57.274
you should be ready to implement
AdditivePhylogeny for yourself.

00:07:01.277 --> 00:07:05.276
Unfortunately though, when we go to apply
this algorithm to the spike protein

00:07:05.276 --> 00:07:09.650
distance matrix taken from coronaviruses,
we find that the matrix is not additive;

00:07:10.785 --> 00:07:13.820
i.e, somewhere along the way,
if you apply AdditivePhylogeny,

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:16.130
you're going to get a negative edge
weight and things are going to go wrong.

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:21.542
So, for now,
let's just fudge this matrix a little bit,

00:07:21.542 --> 00:07:25.157
to yield the following
matrix that's additive.

00:07:25.157 --> 00:07:27.453
Just from this distance matrix,

00:07:27.453 --> 00:07:33.196
I would hope that we should have a pretty
strong guess which animal gave us SARS

00:07:33.196 --> 00:07:36.420
because there's one species
that's much closer to humans than

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:37.920
the rest of the species.

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:43.500
Of course, we should be wary
of jumping to this conclusion

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:47.200
because we already know that the smallest
element of a distance matrix

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:50.080
doesn't necessarily correspond
to neighboring leaves.

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:55.833
But when we run AdditivePhylogeny
on this adjusted distance matrix,

00:07:55.833 --> 00:07:58.381
our suspicions are indeed confirmed.

00:07:58.381 --> 00:08:02.639
The animal that gave us SARS is
the palm civet, a creature so

00:08:02.639 --> 00:08:06.058
fearsome that I hesitate
to even show it to you.

00:08:09.263 --> 00:08:14.630
Now, despite the undeniable cuteness
of the palm civet in this photo,

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:19.610
it is indigenous to Southern China where
it is harvested for its meat.

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:23.880
So it does make sense that at
some point during the process,

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:27.420
the palm civet infected us with SARS, and
that's how it crossed the species barrier.

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:34.310
Additional research has revealed, though,
that the palm civet coronavirus is very

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:39.640
similar to some coronaviruses found in
bats, which are notorious disease carriers.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:43.589
So it is very possible that bats gave
SARS to palm civets, which then passed

00:08:43.589 --> 00:08:44.764
the disease on to us.

00:08:47.004 --> 00:08:50.699
I'd also like to just make a brief aside
that you may have heard about the palm

00:08:50.699 --> 00:08:52.680
civet for a slightly different reason.

00:08:54.530 --> 00:08:57.250
It feeds on raw coffee cherries,

00:08:57.250 --> 00:09:01.480
and some people say that when the beans
pass through its digestive tract,

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:04.720
certain proteins get broken down

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:07.090
that reduce the bitter
flavor of the coffee.

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:12.860
For this reason,
these predigested coffee beans,

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:16.330
after they pass through the palm civet,
have become somewhat of a luxury item.

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:22.460
Colloquially called "cat
poop coffee", this type of coffee sells for

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:23.830
hundreds of dollars per kilogram.

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:29.010
But regardless of what your
morning coffee tastes like,

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:33.660
the fact remains that we had to fudge the
coronavirus distance matrix a little bit

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:35.380
in order to get an additive matrix.

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:40.390
In fact, the majority of distance
matrices that we encounter in

00:09:40.390 --> 00:09:43.140
practice are going to be non-additive.

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:46.860
So our question is, how can we construct
an evolutionary tree for

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:48.640
these non-additive matrices?

